WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.650
have a word file system may be

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:03.690
written without a space as i've done

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.640
here which is pretty common in the

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.070
linux community or it can be written

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.110
with a space the spell checkers little

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.370
happier if you write it with a

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.994
space but either way is fine it

00:00:14.994 --> 00:00:15.840
means the same thing

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.380
and what file systems are is they

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.410
are data structures that are responsible for

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.500
laying out the data on a persistent

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.350
storage device so what we have with

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.470
a mass storage device persistent storage is

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.040
one giant block of storage space we

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.020
partition that block of storage space

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.470
sep into different uses and different partitions

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.290
but each of those partitions is itself

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:46.200
just a block of space the file

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.630
systems goal is to bring order to

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.700
that block of space and make it

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.210
possible for us to easily locate read

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.520
and write data within that space

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.660
organize that data into hierarchies which you

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.730
can think of as folders to make

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.730
it easier for humans to find stuff

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.950
if all your files including all the

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.140
operating system files were in one folder

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.270
it would take forever to find anything

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.150
so we like to do folder organization

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.340
as well as provide

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.850
ways that we pathetic humans can understand

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.070
what's in a file based upon its

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:23.610
name

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.510
file systems also give us the ability

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.970
to share storage among multiple different users

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.190
on a computer system in multiple different

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:36.390
programs things like file permissions allow two

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.450
different users to share one system without

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.310
the risk that they're going to overwrite

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:42.330
each other's files

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.160
one of the most important things that

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.370
file system store along with whatever data

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.250
the user wants to store his information

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.040
about that data said data about data

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:57.900
which we call meta data

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.170
in this meta data includes a wide

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.990
variety of stuff includes the name of

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.900
the file if the file system supports

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.900
permissions it'll include information about the owner

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.450
of the file what group that file

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:16.500
belongs to what it's permissions are it'll

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.260
also contain information about the size of

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.810
the file how big it is as

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.420
well as some timestamps the most common

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.820
timestamps are the creation time the time

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.980
the file was first created and the

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.110
modification time which is the time the

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.380
file was last written some file systems

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.750
also store access times which is the

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.160
time that the file was last read
